nges and the  main changes we see are to do with  pigment so this is things like age spots  so unsurprisingly the best way to  prevent these changes  is using sun protection the obvious  thing to use is sunscreen  and you're looking for a sunscreen that  has high uva and uvb protection  both of these contribute to aging and so  you're looking for a broad spectrum high  spf sunscreen that you can happily wear  every day  one study actually found that daily  sunscreen use actually reversed the  signs of aging  and i think this goes to show that your  skin is  living it has the ability to start  repairing itself  as long as you protect it from getting  reattached every day  in general it's a good idea to wear  sunscreen every day when you go outside  if you're worried about these  extrinsic changes in your skin when  you're indoors there's obviously less uv  uvb doesn't penetrate glass but  depending on your situation  you can still get a large amount of uva  if you're close to a sunny window every  day then it is still a good idea to wear  sunscreen  indoors i have a video about working out  whether or not you need to wear  sunscreen indoors and i'll link that in  the description  now when you're in a car i guess you're  technically indoors  but there are windows everywhere so it  is one of the highest exposures you can  get while technically indoors  there's this really famous photo of a  truck driver where one side of his face  was next to a window the other side was  on  the other side facing away from the  closer window and you can really see the  difference when you're really close to a  window  i've had some people ask me about  fluorescent lights fluorescent lights  can produce  tiny amounts of uv and this is  especially the case with compact  fluorescent bulbs these are the windy  looking ones  because of all those curves the coating  inside the light bulb  that usually absorbs the uv sometimes  gets cracked and so little amounts of uv  come  out but it is still a really tiny amount  of uv  so if you're more than 25 centimeters  away from the light  if you're more than about a foot it  takes about eight hours to get the  equivalent of  just over a minute of midday sun  exposure from somewhere like sydney or  melbourne  if you have a plastic diffuser over the  top like you would in a normal office  fluorescent light  then you get even less uv so for the  vast majority of us  that amount of uv isn't worth worrying  about if you have a photosensitizing  condition like some types of lupus  then it could be significant now one of  the problems with sunscreen  is that we tend to get a lot of wrinkles  near our eyes first  but sunscreen near your eyes can really  sting  i've been adding eye sting into my  sunscreen reviews but i found that some  sunscreens that don't sting my eyes will  sting other people's eyes and vice versa  in general alcohol and sunscreens and  chemical sunscreens  tend to sting people's eyes a bit more  one thing i found really helpful is  mineral sunscreen sticks like this one  from neutrogena  sticks tend to run a lot less because  they have a lot more waxes in them  they're a lot more water resistant  and so this can really help if you want  to get sunscreen around your eyes but  you don't want to risk it running into  your eyes  with sunscreen it's important to  remember that it's not just your face  that gets a lot of sun exposure it's  also your neck and your hands  so if you have a sunscreen that doesn't  really work for your face  you can still use it up by putting it on  your neck and your hands  and remember sun protection isn't just  about sunscreen there are other things  you can do as well  so for example avoid going out into the  direct sun for too long in the middle of  the day when there's the most uva and  uvb  you can also use uv protective clothing  like hats and gloves  i actually keep a uv protective cardigan  in the car because  a lot of the time i forget to take it  with me but also